canonical_claim.statement:
[As printed, p. 2240] "Many familiar linear estimators are unbiased in settings with outcomes that are neither independent nor identically distributed. For example, consider the linear regression model $\mathrm{E}[Y_i] = \sum_{j=1}^{p}x_{ij}\beta_j$ for $i = 1, \ldots, n$ [e.g., 10, Ch. 1], where $x_i$ and $\beta$ have dimension $p \times 1$, with the coefficients $\beta$ estimated by ordinary least squares. Let $X$ be the $n \times p$ matrix of covariates, and let $Y$ be the $n \times 1$ vector of outcomes. The estimated coefficients are $\hat\beta = (X'X)^{-1}X'Y$." [p. 2241] "Define the $p \times n$ matrix $\Theta = n(X'X)^{-1}X'$. Then for $j = 1, \ldots, p$ we can express the vector coefficient estimates as $\hat\beta = n^{-1}\Theta Y$, or individually as $\hat\beta_j = \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Theta_{ji}y_i$, as in Definition 3." "$\mathrm{var}(\hat\beta) = \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i \in \mathcal V_S}^{n}\sum_{j \in \mathcal V_S}^{n}\theta_i\theta_j\,\mathrm{cov}(Y_i, Y_j)$."
[The claims] For a fixed $n \times p$ matrix $X$ with $X'X$ invertible:
(a) $\hat\beta_j = \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Theta_{ji}Y_i$ for $j = 1, \ldots, p$;
(b) if $\mathrm{E}[Y] = X\beta$ then $\mathrm{E}[\hat\beta_j] = \beta_j$, so $\hat\beta_j$ is a linear unbiased estimator of $\beta_j$ in the sense of Definition 3 with $\theta_i = \Theta_{ji}$, whatever the dependence among the $Y_i$ and whether or not they are identically distributed;
(c) for fixed coefficients $\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_n$ and outcomes with finite second moments, $\mathrm{var}\left(\frac1n\sum_i\theta_iY_i\right) = \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\theta_i\theta_j\,\mathrm{cov}(Y_i, Y_j)$.
